WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:09.364
A 77 gigahertz radar needs gain that plain CMOS cannot give, and converters that must stay quiet.

00:00:09.364 --> 00:00:13.132
So SKU-7 splits the work across two dies.

00:00:14.182 --> 00:00:16.442
It starts with a frequency ramp.

00:00:16.442 --> 00:00:23.293
If that ramp bends anywhere across its sweep, the radar starts seeing targets that are not there.

00:00:24.343 --> 00:00:30.356
The oscillator runs at half the carrier, and a doubler lifts it to 77 gigahertz.

00:00:30.356 --> 00:00:33.964
The same chirp then serves transmit and receive.

00:00:35.014 --> 00:00:37.317
Four receivers mix each echo down.

00:00:37.317 --> 00:00:42.736
Frequency tells you distance, and the phase across the four tells you direction.

00:00:43.786 --> 00:00:51.445
The signal crosses between the dies exactly once, as four channels, and becomes a map of range against speed.

00:00:52.495 --> 00:00:59.642
Detection then holds a constant false-alarm rate against clutter, and beamforming adds direction to every target.

00:01:00.692 --> 00:01:08.116
The vehicle gets a list of up to 64 tracked targets, never the raw radar samples behind them.

00:01:09.166 --> 00:01:15.730
SKU-7 is pre-silicon, and its front end can only be proven on silicon.

00:01:15.730 --> 00:01:18.168
There is no FPGA stand-in.

00:01:18.168 --> 00:01:21.732
Bring us your field of view and range.

00:01:21.732 --> 00:01:25.389
We will tell you what SKU-7 must prove.
